$ trilium 
trilium - work with Trilium Notes from the command line

Everyday
  search <query>          Find notes (Trilium search syntax)
  ls [note]               List child notes
  get <note>...           Show a note's details and content
  create <title> [text]   Create a note (default parent: @inbox)
  append <note> [text]    Add content to the end of a note
  edit <note>             Replace exact text in a note
  put <note> [text]       Replace a note's whole content
  rm <note>...            Delete notes

Organize
  update  attr  mv  branch  att (attachments)  rev (revisions)

More
  export  import  history  undelete  info  backup

<note> can be a note ID, "root", or a shortcut: @inbox @today @2026-10-25
Long content: use -f FILE (or -f - for stdin).
Details: trilium help <command>   Search syntax: trilium help search

$ trilium --version
trilium 1.2.3 (commit abc1234)

$ trilium version
trilium 1.2.3 (commit abc1234)

$ trilium help get
Usage: trilium get <note>...

Show each note's metadata header followed by its raw content (HTML for text
notes). Binary content is never printed; save it with -o FILE.
--text shows a read-only plain-text view of text notes: don't use it for edit --old.

Flags:
      --all-attrs       also list inherited attributes
  -c, --content         print only the raw content
  -m, --meta            print only the metadata header
  -o, --output string   save the raw content to FILE (- for stdout)
      --text            plain-text view of text notes (read-only)

Examples:
  trilium get <id1> <id2>
  trilium get <id> -c            # raw content only
  trilium get <id> -o photo.png  # save content to a file

$ trilium append --help
Usage: trilium append <note> [content]

Add content to the end of a note (or the start with --top).
Text notes (HTML) are joined byte for byte. For other notes (code etc.) a
newline is inserted when needed so that lines don't run together.

Flags:
  -f, --file string   read content from FILE (- for stdin)
      --force         allow changing Trilium system notes
      --top           add to the start instead of the end

Examples:
  trilium append @today "<p>Called Alice about the budget.</p>"
  trilium append <id> -f section.html
  trilium append <code-note> -f - --top < header.txt

